<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mirian III of Iberia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mirian III was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, or Kartli, in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in the early 4th century and the first Christian ruler of the country. Georgian tradition, which echoes an account by the church historian Rufinus, holds that he and his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanaya">Nana</a> were converted through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nino">Saint Nino</a> in the 320s or 330s. He is regarded as founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosroid-dynasty">Chosroid dynasty</a> and is venerated as a saint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mirian-iii-of-iberia.jpg" alt="Mirian III of Iberia" width="320" height="600" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kober · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Mirian,_a_17th-century_fresco_from_Svetitskhoveli_Cathedral_(Mtskheta,_Georgia).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mirian is the Georgian form of the Iranian name Mihrān, which Greek transliterates as Mithranes and which the Roman historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a> gives as Meribanes. Mirian belonged to the House of Mihran, one of the Seven Great Houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. He was born in Iran and was originally a Zoroastrian.</p><p>The Sasanian King of Kings Bahram II, who ruled from 274 to 293, secured the Iberian throne for him. Bahram wished to strengthen Sasanian authority in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and to use the Iberian capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtskheta">Mtskheta</a> as an entrance to the passes of the Caucasus Mountains. Mirian was then only seven, and Bahram sent a Mihranid grandee named Mirvanoz as his guardian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Life of the Kings recounts his reign in detail, though its claims that he pretended to the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, controlled Colchis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> and operated as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> are obviously fictional. As a Sasanian vassal he most likely took part in the brief war of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narseh">Narseh</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 297 and 298, which ended in a crushing Sasanian defeat and the cession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Mirian adapted quickly and established close ties with Rome.</p><p>According to tradition, Mirian became a Christian through the ministry of Nino, a Cappadocian missionary. A legend says that while hunting near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtskheta">Mtskheta</a> he was blinded by darkness, which lifted only when he prayed to Nino's God. His second wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanaya">Nana</a>, is said to have converted first. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> became the state religion, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> the second kingdom after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> to do so. The monarchy confiscated the property of pagan temples and gave it to the nobles and the church, and it met resistance from the mountain people. Rufinus and the Georgian annals report that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a> asked Constantine for clergy, and that he sent priests and holy relics.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Georgian tradition relates that Mirian built a cathedral at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtskheta">Mtskheta</a> and made a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> shortly before his death. He and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanaya">Nana</a> are said to be buried at the Samtavro convent in Mtskheta. Nana bore him two sons, Rev and Varaz-Bakur, and a daughter. He is regarded as the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosroid-dynasty">Chosroid dynasty</a>, a branch of the Mihranid family, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgian-orthodox-church">Georgian Orthodox Church</a> honours him as a saint, Equal to the Apostles.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mirian III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Iberia was a hereditary monarchy, ruled in turn by dynasties that Georgian tradition calls the Pharnavazids, Arsacids and, from <b>Mirian III</b>, the Chosroids, although the lists of early kings are partly legendary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kingdom of Iberia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtskheta">Mtskheta</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.84,44.72&amp;year=364" aria-label="Mtskheta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5638 673 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Mirian III of Iberia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6088 943h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6088 943h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.84,44.72&amp;year=364">What was here in 364 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
